{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>To a foreigner you may lend at interest, but to your brother you shall not lend at interest.</b> It is difficult: why did it repeat to say <b>but to your brother you shall not lend at interest</b>, after it commanded just before ״you shall not lend at interest to your brother״ etc.? And our Sages of blessed memory said (Bava Metzi'a 75b) that it is to transgress thereby in two negative prohibitions. Or perhaps it intended to say that even when the lending at interest is to a gentile, if the matter is done through an Israelite it is forbidden — as long as the Israelite does not know the gentile on behalf of his loan, or if the gentile's pledge is not in his hand. And so is the law." "<b>Further,</b> it alludes to what the later authorities distinguished (Yoreh De'ah, siman 159), that even to a gentile it is forbidden to make a practice of this, lest one come to do so also to an Israelite. And that is its saying <b>to a foreigner you may lend at interest</b> — it is a condition: that you not do so in a manner that results in corruption, that you lend at interest to your brother."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:18:46.240963416Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}